<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veleda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 69–77 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Veleda was a seeress of the Bructeri in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the 1st century CE, described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>. Living in a tower near the River Lippe, she foretold the success of the Batavian revolt of 69 and was consulted by other peoples, who sent her gifts and captured Roman ships. Roman sources suggest that she was later captured and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veleda.jpg" alt="Veleda" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augustins_-_Velleda_-_Laurent_Marqueste_2004_1_431.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
